  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 21, $j$6.1 Research: Finding and Evaluating Sources$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Proverbs 2 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Proverbs/2"}], "body": "Research begins with a question. A good research question is focused (not too broad), researchable (answers can be found) and meaningful. Researchers search for information in books, databases, reference works, reliable websites and interviews. Then they evaluate each source with five questions: Who wrote it, and are they qualified (authority)? Is the information correct and supported (accuracy)? Is it recent enough for the topic (currency)? Does the author have a bias or something to sell (purpose)? Does it truly answer my question (relevance)?\n\nA primary source is a first hand record, such as a diary, letter, speech, photograph or interview. A secondary source explains or interprets primary sources, such as a textbook or a biography. Websites ending in .gov and .edu are often reliable, though every source should be checked.\n\nScripture honors the careful search for wisdom. Proverbs 2:4 says, \"If thou seekest her as silver, and searchest for her as for hid treasures;\" Read Proverbs 2 in the King James Version.\n\nYour task: (1) Improve this broad question: \"What about the ocean?\" (2) Tell which of these is a primary source and which is secondary: a soldier's letter home, a history textbook chapter. (3) Name the five questions for evaluating a source and apply two of them to a website you know.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.C.4.1, ELA.K12.EE.5.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.C.4.1, ELA.K12.EE.5.1", "script": "Listen, the world is full of information, but not all of it is true. A good researcher is like a treasure hunter who also knows the difference between gold and glitter. Today we learn to ask a good question, find sources, and test them.\n\nResearch begins with a question. A good research question is focused, so it is not too broad. It is researchable, so answers can actually be found. And it is meaningful, so you care about the answer. What about the ocean is too broad. How do ocean currents affect the weather in Florida is focused and researchable.\n\nNow, where do we look? Books, library databases, reference works, reliable websites and interviews. And we sort sources into two kinds. A primary source is a first hand record. A letter, a diary, a speech, a photograph, an interview. A secondary source explains or interprets primary sources. A textbook, a biography, a news analysis. A soldier's letter home is primary. A history textbook chapter about the same war is secondary. Both are valuable, for different reasons.\n\nWatch this. Then we test every source with five questions. Authority. Who wrote it, and are they qualified? Accuracy. Is the information correct and backed by evidence? Currency. Is it recent enough for my topic? Purpose. Does the author have a bias, or something to sell? And relevance. Does it truly answer my question? A site that ends in dot gov or dot edu is often reliable, but we still check. A site that exists only to sell a product has a purpose that is not to teach.\n\nScripture honors the careful search. Proverbs chapter two, verse four says, If thou seekest her as silver, and searchest for her as for hid treasures. The writer says to search for wisdom the way a miner searches for silver. Be patient, be thorough and be honest about what you find.\n\nOne more habit. Use more than one source. If three reliable sources agree, your confidence grows. If one source stands alone, be careful.\n\nYour task today. Improve the broad question. Label the primary and secondary sources. Then name the five questions and apply two of them to a website you know. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nThe truth is worth searching for. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.1 Research: Finding and Evaluating Sources$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 22, $j$6.2 Citing Sources and Avoiding Plagiarism$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Exodus 20 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Exodus/20"}], "body": "Plagiarism is using another person's words or ideas as if they were your own. It is a form of dishonesty, and it can have serious consequences in school and in life. You avoid it by giving credit. There are three ways to use a source: a direct quotation (exact words in quotation marks), a paraphrase (the idea in your own words and sentence structure, still credited), and a summary (the main points in fewer words, still credited).\n\nA citation tells the reader where information came from. A basic works cited entry lists the author, the title, the publisher or website, and the date. A short in text citation, such as (Lopez 12), points to the entry. Always cite quotations, statistics, paraphrased ideas and any information that is not common knowledge.\n\nScripture teaches honesty and respect for what belongs to another. Exodus 20:15 says, \"Thou shalt not steal.\" Read Exodus 20 in the King James Version.\n\nOriginal text: \"Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of all marine species.\"\nWeak (too close): Coral reefs cover under one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of marine species.\nBetter paraphrase: Although reefs take up a tiny part of the sea floor, a large share of ocean life depends on them (Lopez 12).\n\nYour task: (1) Explain why the weak version is plagiarism. (2) Write your own paraphrase of the original with a citation. (3) Write a works cited entry for a book using these facts: Author Maria Lopez; title Life on the Reef; publisher Harbor Press; year 2021.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.C.4.1, ELA.10.C.5.1, ELA.K12.EE.5.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.C.4.1, ELA.10.C.5.1, ELA.K12.EE.5.1", "script": "Now, let us talk about honesty in research. Everything we write that comes from someone else needs a name on it. That is called giving credit, and failing to do it is called plagiarism.\n\nPlagiarism is using another person's words or ideas as if they were your own. It does not matter whether you did it on purpose. It is a form of dishonesty, and the consequences can be serious. A failing grade. A lost scholarship. A damaged reputation. The good news is that plagiarism is easy to avoid when you understand three ways to use a source.\n\nFirst, a direct quotation. You use the exact words, in quotation marks, with a citation. Second, a paraphrase. You put the idea in your own words and your own sentence structure, and you still give credit. Third, a summary. You give the main points in fewer words, and you still give credit.\n\nWatch this. Changing three words is not a paraphrase. It is a copy with a disguise. Look at the lesson. The original says coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of all marine species. The weak version says coral reefs cover under one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of marine species. The structure is the same, and only a few words were swapped. That is too close. The better version says, although reefs take up a tiny part of the sea floor, a large share of ocean life depends on them, and then it adds the citation, Lopez twelve. The idea is the same, but the sentence is truly mine, and the credit is given.\n\nA citation tells the reader where the information came from. A basic works cited entry lists the author, the title, the publisher and the date. Lopez, Maria. Life on the Reef. Harbor Press, 2021. And the short citation in the text, Lopez twelve, points the reader to that entry. Cite quotations, statistics, paraphrased ideas and anything that is not common knowledge.\n\nScripture gives us the foundation. Exodus chapter twenty, verse fifteen says, Thou shalt not steal. Ideas are a kind of treasure. To honor the owner is to honor God, who commanded honesty.\n\nYour task today. Explain why the weak version is plagiarism. Write your own paraphrase with a citation. Then write the works cited entry. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nIntegrity is excellence that no one sees. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.2 Citing Sources and Avoiding Plagiarism$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 23, $j$6.3 Speaking, Listening and Presenting$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Ecclesiastes 3 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Ecclesiastes/3"}], "body": "A strong presentation is planned, practiced and delivered with care. Plan with the audience and purpose in mind. Organize the talk with an opening that gets attention, a clear main message, supporting points with evidence, and a closing that is easy to remember. Use visuals, such as slides, charts or objects, only to support the talk, with few words and large type.\n\nDelivery matters: speak clearly and at a steady pace, make eye contact, stand with good posture, use natural gestures and vary your voice. Avoid filler words such as \"um\" and \"like.\" Practice aloud at least three times and keep within the time limit.\n\nListening is also a skill. Active listeners give full attention, take brief notes, ask thoughtful questions, and respond with respect even when they disagree. In a group discussion, build on others' ideas and share the floor.\n\nEcclesiastes 3:7 says there is \"a time to keep silence, and a time to speak.\" Read Ecclesiastes 3 in the King James Version.\n\nYour task: (1) Write a one sentence attention getting opening for a three minute talk on a topic of your choice. (2) List three supporting points. (3) Write one respectful question or response you could offer after hearing someone else's presentation.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.C.2.1, ELA.10.C.5.2, ELA.K12.EE.6.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.C.2.1, ELA.10.C.5.2, ELA.K12.EE.6.1", "script": "Now, let us talk about speaking and listening. Many people fear public speaking more than almost anything. But speaking is a skill, and skills can be learned. With planning and practice, you can stand before a room and serve your listeners well.\n\nFirst, plan. Who is my audience? What is my purpose? To inform, to persuade or to inspire? Then organize. A strong talk has an opening that gets attention, a clear main message, supporting points with evidence, and a closing that is easy to remember. A question, a short story, or a surprising fact can open a talk. A good closing returns to the opening and leaves the audience with one thought.\n\nVisuals are helpful, but they are servants, not stars. Use few words, large type and clear images. Never read your slides word for word. The audience came to hear you.\n\nWatch this. Delivery matters as much as content. Speak clearly and at a steady pace. Nervous speakers rush, so slow down. Make eye contact with different people in the room. Stand tall. Use natural gestures. Vary your voice, so that important words sound important. Avoid filler words like um and like. A pause is better than a filler. And practice out loud, at least three times, with a timer. You will find the parts that need to change.\n\nNow, listening. Listening is not waiting for your turn to talk. An active listener gives full attention, takes brief notes, and asks thoughtful questions. When you disagree, respond with respect. In a group discussion, build on what others say. You might begin with, I like your point about, and I would add. And share the floor, so everyone has a voice.\n\nScripture reminds us of wisdom in timing. Ecclesiastes chapter three, verse seven says, a time to keep silence, and a time to speak. A wise person knows both. Speak when you have something true and helpful to say, and listen when someone else does.\n\nYour task today. Write a one sentence attention getting opening for a three minute talk. List three supporting points. And write one respectful question or response you could offer after someone else's presentation. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nYour voice is a gift. Use it to build up. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.3 Speaking, Listening and Presenting$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 24, $j$6.4 Vocabulary: Context Clues, Word Parts and Word Choice$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Proverbs 4 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Proverbs/4"}], "body": "A strong vocabulary helps you read difficult texts and write with precision. You can figure out new words in three ways.\n\nContext clues: The words around an unfamiliar word give hints. Types include definition (\"A benefactor, someone who gives money or help, visited the school.\"), example, contrast (\"Unlike his generous brother, he was frugal.\") and inference.\n\nWord parts: Many English words are built from prefixes (at the beginning), roots (the core) and suffixes (at the end). Prefix examples: un (not), re (again), pre (before), mis (wrongly). Root examples: bene (good, as in benefit), dict (say, as in predict), spect (look, as in inspect), port (carry, as in transport). Suffix examples: able (can be done), ment (result of an action), ful (full of).\n\nWord choice: Precise words are better than vague ones. Instead of \"said,\" a writer may choose \"whispered,\" \"declared\" or \"replied.\" Notice connotation, the feelings a word carries: \"thrifty\" is positive, while \"stingy\" is negative.\n\nProverbs 4:7 says, \"Wisdom is the principal thing; therefore get wisdom: and with all thy getting get understanding.\" Read Proverbs 4 in the King James Version.\n\nYour task: (1) Use context to explain \"frugal\" in the contrast example above. (2) Break down \"unpredictable\" into prefix, root and suffix and tell what it means. (3) Replace the vague word \"good\" in the sentence, She gave a good speech, with a more precise word.\n\nFlorida standards: ELA.10.V.1.1, ELA.10.V.1.2, ELA.K12.EE.4.1.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "ELA.10.V.1.1, ELA.10.V.1.2, ELA.K12.EE.4.1", "script": "Listen, words are the tools of thought. The more words you own, the more clearly you can think, read and speak. Today we learn three ways to unlock a word you have never seen before.\n\nThe first way is context clues. The words around a strange word often give you hints. Sometimes the sentence gives the definition. A benefactor, someone who gives money or help, visited the school. The phrase after the comma tells you what benefactor means. Sometimes a sentence gives an example. Sometimes it gives a contrast. Unlike his generous brother, he was frugal. The word unlike is a signal. The brother is generous, so frugal must be the opposite. It means careful with money. And sometimes you have to infer, which means you put the clues together and make a smart guess.\n\nWatch this. The second way is word parts. Many English words are built like blocks. A prefix goes at the beginning. A root is the core. A suffix goes at the end. Prefixes such as un mean not, re means again, pre means before, mis means wrongly. Roots such as bene mean good, dict means say, spect means look, port means carry. Suffixes such as able mean can be done, ment means the result of an action, ful means full of.\n\nLet us build one. Unpredictable. The prefix un means not. The root dict, with pre, gives predict, meaning to say beforehand. The suffix able means can be done. So unpredictable means not able to be said beforehand, in other words, you cannot know what will happen. Once you see the parts, a long word becomes simple.\n\nThe third way is word choice. When you write, choose the precise word. Instead of said, you may choose whispered, declared or replied. Instead of good, you might say persuasive, memorable or moving. Also notice connotation, the feelings a word carries. Thrifty and stingy both describe a person who spends little, but thrifty sounds like praise and stingy sounds like blame. A careful writer chooses the word with the right feeling.\n\nScripture treasures understanding. Proverbs chapter four, verse seven says, Wisdom is the principal thing; therefore get wisdom: and with all thy getting get understanding. Words are one of the best ways to get understanding.\n\nYour task today. Use context to explain frugal. Break down unpredictable. And replace the word good in the sentence, she gave a good speech, with a more precise word. Take the four question check when you finish.\n\nA rich vocabulary is a gift that lasts a lifetime. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.4 Vocabulary: Context Clues, Word Parts and Word Choice$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 6, $j$Assessment 6: Research and Present$j$, $j${"prompt": "Complete a short research project and presentation outline on a topic your teacher approves, or choose one of these: an important event in Florida history, a famous inventor, or the history of a Christian school or ministry.\n\nPart one, research (about 300 words): Write a focused research question. Use at least three sources, including at least one primary source and one reliable website or book. Write a short evaluation of each source using authority, accuracy, currency, purpose and relevance. Write a one paragraph summary of what you learned, using at least one direct quotation and one paraphrase, each cited with a short in text citation. Include a works cited list with all three sources.\n\nPart two, presentation plan: Write a three minute talk outline with an attention getting opening, a main message, three supporting points and a memorable closing. List what visuals you would use. Be honest and give credit to every source.", "rubric": "100 points. Focused research question: 10 points. Source selection and evaluation: 25 points. Accurate paraphrase and quotation with citations, no plagiarism: 25 points. Works cited list: 10 points. Presentation outline (opening, main message, supports, closing, visuals): 20 points. Conventions and organization: 10 points. A score of 70 or above is passing."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 6: Research and Present$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 6, $j$Discussion 6: Honest Research, Honest Speech$j$, $j${"prompt": "Why does honesty matter in research and in public speaking? Write a post of at least 120 words that (1) explains what you think is the biggest danger of copying another person's work, (2) names one way to check whether a source can be trusted, (3) shares one thing you can do to become a better speaker or listener, and (4) connects your answer to a verse or principle from Scripture such as Exodus 20:15 or Proverbs 4:7. Then write a respectful reply of at least 40 words to another student's post, or answer this question: How can you disagree with a speaker and still listen well? Keep your language clean and your tone kind."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 6: Honest Research, Honest Speech$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 26, $j$Check 6.1: Research: Finding and Evaluating Sources$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A good research question is:", "options": ["As broad as possible", "A statement", "Impossible to answer", "Focused and researchable"]}, {"q": "A primary source is:", "options": ["An opinion about a topic", "A first hand record such as a letter or speech", "A rumor", "A textbook summary"]}, {"q": "Checking who wrote a source and whether they are qualified tests its:", "options": ["Color", "Authority", "Title", "Length"]}, {"q": "If a website exists mainly to sell a product, you should question its:", "options": ["Number of pictures", "Purpose and possible bias", "Page color", "Font size"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.1: Research: Finding and Evaluating Sources$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,1,1,1}'::int[], array[$j$A good question is focused and can be answered with research.$j$,$j$Primary sources come from the time or the person involved.$j$,$j$Authority asks who is speaking and whether they are qualified.$j$,$j$A seller may have a bias.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.1: Research: Finding and Evaluating Sources$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 27, $j$Check 6.2: Citing Sources and Avoiding Plagiarism$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "Plagiarism is:", "options": ["Using another person's words or ideas without credit", "Writing an outline", "Summarizing with credit", "Quoting with a citation"]}, {"q": "A paraphrase is:", "options": ["The idea restated in your own words, with credit", "An exact quote", "A copy with three words changed", "A list of page numbers"]}, {"q": "Exact words from a source must be placed in:", "options": ["A footer", "Bold letters only", "Quotation marks with a citation", "Parentheses only"]}, {"q": "A works cited entry normally includes:", "options": ["Only your name", "Only the page color", "A drawing", "Author, title, publisher and date"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.2: Citing Sources and Avoiding Plagiarism$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,0,2,3}'::int[], array[$j$Plagiarism is using someone's work without credit.$j$,$j$A paraphrase uses your own words and structure and still cites the source.$j$,$j$Direct quotations need quotation marks and a citation.$j$,$j$These details let the reader find the source.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.2: Citing Sources and Avoiding Plagiarism$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 28, $j$Check 6.3: Speaking, Listening and Presenting$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A strong opening for a talk should:", "options": ["List every source", "Apologize for being nervous", "Read the whole script", "Get the audience's attention"]}, {"q": "Visuals in a presentation should:", "options": ["Be hard to read", "Support the talk with few words and clear images", "Contain every word you say", "Replace the speaker"]}, {"q": "Which is a sign of active listening?", "options": ["Taking brief notes and asking thoughtful questions", "Looking at a phone", "Planning your reply only", "Interrupting often"]}, {"q": "The best way to improve a presentation is to:", "options": ["Memorize without practice", "Avoid eye contact", "Practice aloud with a timer", "Wait until the last minute"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.3: Speaking, Listening and Presenting$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,1,0,2}'::int[], array[$j$An opening should draw the listeners in.$j$,$j$Visuals support the speaker.$j$,$j$Active listeners attend and respond.$j$,$j$Practice reveals problems and builds confidence.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.3: Speaking, Listening and Presenting$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 29, $j$Check 6.4: Vocabulary: Context Clues, Word Parts and Word Choice$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "In 'Unlike his generous brother, he was frugal,' the word unlike signals a:", "options": ["Question", "Contrast", "Rhyme", "Definition"]}, {"q": "The prefix 'un' usually means:", "options": ["Over", "Again", "Not", "Before"]}, {"q": "The root 'spect' means:", "options": ["Carry", "Say", "Look", "Good"]}, {"q": "Which word has the most positive connotation?", "options": ["Stingy", "Tight", "Cheap", "Thrifty"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.4: Vocabulary: Context Clues, Word Parts and Word Choice$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{1,2,2,3}'::int[], array[$j$Unlike signals that frugal is the opposite of generous.$j$,$j$Un means not.$j$,$j$Spect comes from a word meaning look.$j$,$j$Thrifty is a positive word for careful spending.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.4: Vocabulary: Context Clues, Word Parts and Word Choice$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 30, $j$Unit Quiz 6: Research, Presenting and Language Skills$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A good research question is:", "options": ["Focused and researchable", "A fact", "Impossible to research", "Too broad to answer"]}, {"q": "A diary written during a war is a:", "options": ["Rumor", "Primary source", "Secondary source", "Textbook"]}, {"q": "Asking whether an author is qualified to write about a subject tests:", "options": ["Rhyme", "Color", "Authority", "Length"]}, {"q": "A paraphrase must be:", "options": ["In your own words and credited", "In quotation marks without a citation", "Left without credit", "A copy with three words changed"]}, {"q": "Using another person's words without credit is:", "options": ["A paraphrase", "Plagiarism", "A summary with credit", "A citation"]}, {"q": "A strong presentation closing should be:", "options": ["Silent", "Easy to remember", "A list of apologies", "Longer than the talk"]}, {"q": "The root 'dict' means:", "options": ["Say", "Carry", "Look", "Good"]}, {"q": "Exodus 20:15 says, 'Thou shalt not steal.' This connects to research by teaching:", "options": ["Honesty about whose ideas and words belong to whom", "That sources do not matter", "That quotes are never allowed", "That research is not needed"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 6: Research, Presenting and Language Skills$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,1,2,0,1,1,0,0}'::int[], array[$j$A good question can be answered with careful research.$j$,$j$A diary is a first hand record.$j$,$j$Authority concerns who wrote the source.$j$,$j$A paraphrase uses your own words and still cites the source.$j$,$j$That is plagiarism.$j$,$j$A memorable closing leaves the audience with one thought.$j$,$j$Dict means say.$j$,$j$Giving credit honors the owner of the work.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 6: Research, Presenting and Language Skills$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
